        "section": [
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">COPD exacerbation, treated with bronchodilators and steroids</div>"
            },
            "code": {
              "coding": [
                {
                  "code": "67781-5",
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear Colleague, I am writing to inform you about the recent hospital admission of your 78-year-old male patient, who presented with an acute exacerbation of his known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. On admission, he exhibited marked dyspnoea, reduced oxygen saturation, and increased sputum production. Chest X-ray confirmed hyperinflation without pneumonia. Arterial blood gas analysis revealed hypoxaemic respiratory insufficiency. Blood tests showed mild systemic inflammation. The patient was promptly initiated on supplemental oxygen therapy, nebulised bronchodilators, and a course of systemic corticosteroids. His condition improved steadily over the two-day admission. At discharge, oxygen saturation was stable on room air. I recommend continuation of optimised inhaler therapy and close outpatient follow-up. Patient education regarding exacerbation recognition and management was provided prior to discharge.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
              "coding": [
